WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.868 --> 00:00:01.934
-Whoo!
-Whoo!

00:00:01.934 --> 00:00:03.667
-John Stamos, you are the best.

00:00:03.667 --> 00:00:05.701
We have a lot to talk about.
It is out.

00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:08.000
It is out today officially.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.701
Your memoir.

00:00:09.701 --> 00:00:11.501
We talked about this
-Yes.

00:00:11.501 --> 00:00:12.701
-And now it's finally out.

00:00:12.701 --> 00:00:13.734
We can discuss things.

00:00:13.734 --> 00:00:14.934
-We can talk about
so many things

00:00:14.934 --> 00:00:16.000
-We can talk about it.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.534
So first of all,
happy belated birthday.

00:00:17.534 --> 00:00:19.100
-Thank you.
-How old?

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.033
-42.
-Wow. Unbelievable.

00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:22.133
Congratulations.

00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:25.501
-I turned 60.
-60-years-old.

00:00:25.501 --> 00:00:26.834
That doesn't make sense to me.

00:00:26.834 --> 00:00:28.200
-Me either.
-It doesn't make sense to you.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.033
-No, it's good.
-I got to say, this book,

00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:33.601
by the way, congratulations on
the reviews coming in.

00:00:33.601 --> 00:00:34.801
You're getting
a lot of praise

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:37.300
because you're very honest
about things.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.901
Your sobriety, your divorce.
-Yeah.

00:00:39.901 --> 00:00:43.400
-It's got to be pretty tough
to write a memoir in some way.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.100
-I didn't -- I didn't -- I never
thought I'd write a book.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.167
It was the last thing
on my mind.

00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:48.934
And, you know,
they just kept asking.

00:00:48.934 --> 00:00:50.400
And then I was
a father at 55.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.834
And then, you know,
Bob Saget died.

00:00:51.834 --> 00:00:54.634
And so I thought, well, maybe
I do have something to say.

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:55.801
And so I started writing it.

00:00:55.801 --> 00:00:58.200
And as I was writing it,
I discovered my story.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.834
I decided to write
the two hardest chapters.

00:00:59.834 --> 00:01:01.767
The first chapter
was terrible,

00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:03.667
my lowest point
where I got a DUI

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:05.434
and my five stages of grief

00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:08.067
there were like sex,
drugs, alcohol.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.534
And then I wrote
the second hardest,

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:12.133
which I thought was
the Bob Saget chapter.

00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:14.033
And by then I was --
my five stages of grief

00:01:14.033 --> 00:01:16.634
were health and therapy
and family.

00:01:16.634 --> 00:01:18.033
And it was, you know,
it was trying

00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:20.934
to figure out how I got there,
but it was a good journey.

00:01:20.934 --> 00:01:22.868
-Amazing stories
throughout the whole thing.

00:01:22.868 --> 00:01:25.567
I mean, what a crazy career
that you've had.

00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:27.601
-I didn't think so,
and then I started writing

00:01:27.601 --> 00:01:29.234
I was like, you know,
the Beach Boys.

00:01:29.234 --> 00:01:31.400
But I wanted to find
relatable issues.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.567
You know, I was bullied
and that's, you know?

00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:34.901
-That's the part
I love that stuff.

00:01:34.901 --> 00:01:36.367
But I also love, I mean,

00:01:36.367 --> 00:01:37.968
I'm a fan
from "General Hospital."

00:01:37.968 --> 00:01:40.000
-Oh, you are?
-Oh, yeah. I mean...

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.334
-They wanted a street urchin,
you know, from New York.

00:01:42.334 --> 00:01:43.534
I'd never been to New York.

00:01:43.534 --> 00:01:44.868
I didn't know what
a street urchin was.

00:01:44.868 --> 00:01:46.634
-So the audition
for "General Hospital."

00:01:46.634 --> 00:01:48.400
-It's like a street guy,
Blackie Parrish, you know?

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.267
-Yeah, exactly.
-Yeah, and I didn't --

00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:52.667
I went to the library
to see what street urchin was.

00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:53.767
-Street urchin.

00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:54.868
-But I figured,
you know, I was in --

00:01:54.868 --> 00:01:56.367
I loved Travolta
like you did, right?

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:59.167
[ Chuckles ]

00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:00.567
-I can't believe...

00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:02.167
[ Both imitating John Travolta ]

00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:03.234
-What's the matter with me?
What's the matter with you?

00:02:03.234 --> 00:02:04.667
-What's the matter with you?

00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:06.133
Oh.
He's the best, right?

00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:07.601
-He's the...
-I loved it.

00:02:07.601 --> 00:02:09.701
So I figured
I need a walk,

00:02:09.701 --> 00:02:12.367
you know, for
the Blackie character.

00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:14.200
And I drove around Hollywood,

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.167
and I went to this one,
Santa Monica Boulevard.

00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:18.767
I thought, this looks
like New York, right?

00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:20.133
I borrowed my mom's
leather jacket,

00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:22.367
and it was kind of puffy,
you know, I thought it was cool,

00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:24.601
but it was like this long,
puffy jacket.

00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:26.934
I had feathered hair,
sun in it like you,

00:02:26.934 --> 00:02:28.934
sun into my hair.

00:02:28.934 --> 00:02:31.167
Did you put sun in?
-Yes, I did.

00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:32.801
I was a Beach Boy.
-Sun in. Why not, man?

00:02:32.801 --> 00:02:35.701
-Yeah, yeah.
And so I walked here --

00:02:35.701 --> 00:02:37.834
And I figured if I put --
Help me.

00:02:37.834 --> 00:02:40.167
Because remember
when he had...

00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:42.167
But I didn't --
I wasn't very good.

00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:44.100
But you know like...
-I used to walk like...

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.200
-Show me how.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.200
So I was trying to, first,

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.400
I was like walking like this

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.167
and I heard a lot of go,
"You got it, girl."

00:02:49.167 --> 00:02:50.434
And I was like, "Girl? Okay."

00:02:50.434 --> 00:02:52.601
And then...
-No, no. Travolta kind of...

00:02:52.601 --> 00:02:53.767
-Yeah.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:57.434 --> 00:03:04.067
♪♪

00:03:04.067 --> 00:03:06.167
[ Laughs ]

00:03:06.167 --> 00:03:07.434
Right?
-Yeah!

00:03:07.434 --> 00:03:09.167
And that
got you the role?

00:03:09.167 --> 00:03:10.167
-Thank you.
Huh?

00:03:10.167 --> 00:03:11.334
-And that got you the role?

00:03:11.334 --> 00:03:13.033
-No, they said
stop moving around so much.

00:03:13.033 --> 00:03:14.567
Just say the lines.
-They said stop stop moving.

00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:16.000
-Yeah, stop moving.
-Yeah.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.567
How do you go
from "General Hospital"

00:03:17.567 --> 00:03:20.000
to being in the Beach Boys?

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.567
-That's a good question.
-I mean, how do you get --

00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:24.434
how do you play
with the Beach Boys?

00:03:24.434 --> 00:03:26.734
-They were playing at
a baseball stadium in San Diego,

00:03:26.734 --> 00:03:30.667
and my friend was in the band,
and he said, now when fun --

00:03:30.667 --> 00:03:33.300
fun -- fun is over,
get back there.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.033
And, you know, and they hang out
and then they go back

00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:36.133
do an encore, then they leave.

00:03:36.133 --> 00:03:37.634
So that's the only time
to meet them.

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:38.734
-If you want to meet them,
that's what you do.

00:03:38.734 --> 00:03:40.901
-Right.
And so fun, fun, fun.

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:42.501
And I said, okay, where do I go?

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:44.033
And I walked down to the field,

00:03:44.033 --> 00:03:45.434
and I was just
on "General Hospital"

00:03:45.434 --> 00:03:47.601
and I heard all this screaming,
screaming.

00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:49.734
I go, "What is that?"
And I go, "Oh, it's for me."

00:03:49.734 --> 00:03:51.400
And all these cheerleaders
started chasing me

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.501
around the diamond
like an idiot.

00:03:53.501 --> 00:03:54.767
I'm like, running the bases.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.300
-They were running after you?
-Yeah.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:57.400
-And they were
catching me

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:58.801
because I wasn't
a very good runner.

00:03:58.801 --> 00:04:01.267
I had skinny legs,
these Jordache jeans,

00:04:01.267 --> 00:04:03.567
my feathered, you know,
I look like I had

00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:05.067
like a dead crow on my head.

00:04:05.067 --> 00:04:07.167
And I'm running, and I look up,
I'm on the jumbotron,

00:04:07.167 --> 00:04:08.601
and these girls.

00:04:08.601 --> 00:04:10.234
And I run, run,
and there's a backstage,

00:04:10.234 --> 00:04:11.567
then I ran in
and they slammed the door

00:04:11.567 --> 00:04:12.868
and these girls were screaming.

00:04:12.868 --> 00:04:14.133
I'm like [panting].
You know?

00:04:14.133 --> 00:04:16.200
Like, all the Beach Boys
turned to look at me like,

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.634
"Who's that weirdo?"
You know?

00:04:17.634 --> 00:04:19.601
And they, you know...
-And that's...

00:04:19.601 --> 00:04:21.000
-Yeah, I was sweating.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.334
-...That John Stamos from
"General Hospital" well.

00:04:22.334 --> 00:04:23.834
-Yeah. My friend said
that's John Stamos.

00:04:23.834 --> 00:04:26.634
Mike Love said, "Do girls scream
for him like that all the time?"

00:04:26.634 --> 00:04:28.767
And he said, "Yeah."
He said, "Get him on stage."

00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:30.367
-Is that right?
-And I played "Barbara Ann"

00:04:30.367 --> 00:04:31.801
and that was -- that was it.
It was beautiful.

00:04:31.801 --> 00:04:36.033
-I will say there's a funny,
funny, funny stories in here.

00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:39.934
There's some cool stories just
for anyone that's growing up

00:04:39.934 --> 00:04:41.501
and you go, yeah,
I can relate to that.

00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:43.367
But then there's also
really touching stories.

00:04:43.367 --> 00:04:46.734
I want to say the last chapter
is our friend,

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:48.400
you're a really good friend
and my good friend too,

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.801
but yours more than me.

00:04:49.801 --> 00:04:51.934
But Bob Saget was your brother.
-Yeah.

00:04:51.934 --> 00:04:53.434
-And that was our --

00:04:53.434 --> 00:04:55.701
that was the last night
that I saw him.

00:04:55.701 --> 00:04:58.067
And you never, you know, you go
to dinner, like,

00:04:58.067 --> 00:04:59.234
you never think
it's going to be

00:04:59.234 --> 00:05:01.167
the last time you see them,
the last picture.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:02.434
And, Jimmy, he was like

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:03.801
everything
that I wanted Bob to be.

00:05:03.801 --> 00:05:07.200
He was calm and, you know,
he listened and he was so sweet.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.467
And we were there
with two girls,

00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:09.701
and we we looked at each other
like, God,

00:05:09.701 --> 00:05:11.701
what did we do
to get these beautiful wives?

00:05:11.701 --> 00:05:13.667
And we were -- it was just
such a beautiful night.

00:05:13.667 --> 00:05:16.734
And, you know, you're always
in a hurry in life, right?

00:05:16.734 --> 00:05:18.234
Oh, I got to go do this,
I got to do that.

00:05:18.234 --> 00:05:19.734
And, you know, we stayed.

00:05:19.734 --> 00:05:21.100
And when something
like that happens,

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.501
you kind of want
to remember everything.

00:05:22.501 --> 00:05:24.000
And what did we eat?
What did we do?

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.868
And we ordered some cake
and we had some coffee.

00:05:26.868 --> 00:05:30.100
And we stayed longer than we
normally would have, you know?

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.601
And so if you have a chance,
if you're with someone you love,

00:05:33.601 --> 00:05:35.734
someone you care about,
stay a little longer,

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:37.234
have the cake, have some coffee.

00:05:37.234 --> 00:05:38.934
And it was a beautiful night.

00:05:38.934 --> 00:05:42.834
And, you know,
it's hard without him.

00:05:42.834 --> 00:05:44.100
[ Applause ]

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.300
-Thank you for doing it, buddy.
Thank you, bud.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:49.067
Thank you for coming on.
Congratulations on the book.

00:05:49.067 --> 00:05:51.133
We love you, buddy.
John Stamos.

00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:53.033
"If You Would Have Told Me"

00:05:53.033 --> 00:05:54.400
is available everywhere
right now.

